    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1664809-038:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "European Boards of Directors and company lawyers should fol- low upcoming events closely and prepare themselves for these impending changes. They should also ask Congress to reconsider",
      "text_sha256": "d6c89dbd2ca068076bdeae7c7a6cc50ab4ec5a83a6ca95f302258b1cef338300",
      "source_sha256": "2d5c8c30f969f8eb613bfa1fe6888a7cfa1a452e1027bcda0489618ceac62c20",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-003",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-003",
      "text": "The plaintiffs' reading that Section 10(b) covers transactions anywhere in any security of a class listed on a U.S. exchange is inconsistent with Morrison, because the opinion turns on the location of the transaction and on the holding that Section 10(b) has no extraterritorial application.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "dually listed securities",
        "transactions executed on a foreign exchange"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-003:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "This interpretation is inconsistent with the basic thrust of the Morrison opin- ion, which focuses on the location of the transactions and the fact that Section 10(b) does not apply extraterritorially,",
      "text_sha256": "75b82a08ba27f0d6b1fc52a26fd5fd0364d093fd027ba38a4209e377832a87a4",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-004",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-004",
      "text": "Morrison itself refutes a listing based reading of Section 10(b): the Court knew that National Australia Bank had registered and listed shares in New York, yet held that Section 10(b) did not reach trades in those ordinary shares in Australia.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "foreign issuers with U.S. registered ADRs or listed shares"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-004:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The entire point of the Morrison opinion, however, is that Section 10(b) did not apply to trades in NAB's ordinary shares in Australia. This is inconsistent with a theory that the Court would apply Section 10(b) to any security listed on a U.S. exchange",
      "text_sha256": "9c47d8112241d40bebb90ba144293ca7e9524078b1b070561c8e02acba0f2e82",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-005",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-005",
      "text": "There is no substantive difference between a foreign issuer listing ADRs on a U.S. exchange and listing its ordinary shares there, so Section 10(b) coverage of foreign transactions should not turn on which arrangement the issuer chose.",
      "claim_type": "definitional",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "foreign issuers accessing U.S. markets through ADRs"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-005:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "It also makes little sense to argue that the result would be different, and Section 10(b) would apply to transactions in NAB's ordinary shares outside the United States, if NAB had listed its ordinary shares for trading on the NYSE instead of listing the ADRs.",
      "text_sha256": "7c78603ed02353eb887089a7d1843c8822b085a91c93d175aa3dd4d530279bd7",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-010",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-010",
      "text": "The Porsche swap transactions were not the functional equivalent of a transaction on a U.S. securities exchange, but neither were they the functional equivalent of a transaction on a German exchange, contrary to the implication of Judge Baer's opinion.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "over-the-counter security-based swap agreements"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-010:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "In the actual Porsche case, however, the swap transactions were not the func- tional equivalent of a transaction on a U.S. securities exchange, even if they also were not the functional equivalent of a transaction on an exchange in Germany.",
      "text_sha256": "3a0133985a51f425db59a7c148b458a44bab638546b3ecaf378393a2a1e86feb",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-013",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-013",
      "text": "If the presence of a single U.S. party sufficed to locate a privately negotiated derivative transaction in the United States, U.S. parties could sue in U.S. courts regardless of other factors such as where the counterparties were located.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "privately negotiated derivative transactions",
        "a one party location test"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-013:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "If the presence of one party alone should suffice, U.S. parties to privately negotiated derivative transactions would be able to sue in U.S. courts regardless of other factors, such as where the counterparties were located,",
      "text_sha256": "d6555c96a07024d0649081922da56b296a7ddef0ed47745504f47ee8a3ebd1d8",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-014",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-014",
      "text": "Locating a private transaction by the location of the parties is often unworkable, because the parties are frequently institutions simultaneously present in the United States and abroad, and individual parties are often in transit.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "private transactions in conventional securities",
        "institutional and mobile parties"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-014:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Parties to a private transaction are often institutions having a simultaneous presence in the U.S. and abroad. Individual parties to a private transaction are often in transit.",
      "text_sha256": "a1de5067f02a23f36c69a5bdbd3802beaf610f01ed36386ecedd714b3418f24f",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-017",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/1765901-017",
      "text": "Non-U.S. investors have a strong incentive to route foreign trades through U.S. brokers if that preserves a U.S. cause of action, because their home jurisdictions rarely offer the attractive features of the U.S. system such as broad discovery, higher damages, class actions, and no exposure to defendants' costs.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "a broker location theory accepted by U.S. courts",
        "non-U.S. clients trading non-U.S. securities"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 12
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:1765901-017:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Indeed, the home jurisdiction of non-U.S. clients of U.S. brokers is not likely to provide many of the attractive features of the U.S. legal system, such as unparalleled discovery, a tendency towards higher damages and settle- ment amounts, the availability of securities class actions",
      "text_sha256": "51a123ce5264eb078cbdad68e39ad7271461564733d828cd9401b703bea00b67",
      "source_sha256": "7b08b8a15f7d72b4eeed41be2d17c20428cb4727269077a6575c5d22e31ecdc5",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
