    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-005",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-005",
      "text": "Because most national crisis responses took the form of public bail-outs adopted without broad international consensus, they increased the threat of international regulatory arbitrage and damaged the global competitiveness of national financial markets.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "systemic-risk",
        "regulatory-failure",
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "crisis-era emergency measures adopted without international coordination"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 15
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-005:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Most national measures took the form of public bail-outs without broad international consensus,44 thereby increasing the threat of international regulatory arbitrage as well as negatively impacting the global competitiveness of national financial markets.",
      "text_sha256": "3fa6c13cdfc5124c882be8c4278adfcf4d872a0de092e0e91f99993ec26bdbab",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-006",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-006",
      "text": "Mutual recognition of countercyclical capital buffers hollows out the national discretion the Commission proposal appears to grant: the financially strongest Member States with the largest financial sectors will effectively set the buffer size for smaller Member States whose institutions do business there.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "countercyclical buffers up to the 2.5 percent mutual recognition ceiling",
        "institutions with branches or subsidiaries in the buffer-setting Member State"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-006:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Therefore, the financially stronger Member States with the largest financial sectors may dictate the size of countercyclical buffers for smaller Member States, thereby practically reducing any proposed national discretion.",
      "text_sha256": "54f4159829ab25ed41f62ca35a32dafd2b77fa8a6367f07c07dd44e48cff912c",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-007",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-007",
      "text": "Even the residual national discretion to set countercyclical buffers between 2.5 and 5 percent is misleading, because a Member State such as the United Kingdom is unlikely to sustain a 5 percent buffer while Germany requires only 2.5 percent and thereby hands German banks a competitive advantage.",
      "claim_type": "predictive",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "buffers set above the 2.5 percent mutual recognition ceiling",
        "competing national banking sectors, especially where the state holds bank ownership stakes"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-007:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The Commission proposal explicitly allows Member States to implement countercyclical buffers of up to 5%, but limits mutual recognition to the 2.5% ceiling. Even in these instances, the proposed discretion may be misleading.",
      "text_sha256": "ec0dbcef7dbbbfd6d2cd95d2488539b0492d8ccde7a35a72b16b9f7ee9af229c",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-010",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-010",
      "text": "Reliance on public bail-outs, unaccompanied by any threat that management, shareholders and creditors would share significant losses, created an asymmetric incentive for excessive risk taking by financial institutions.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "systemic-risk"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "Germany and the United States during the height of the financial crisis",
        "where insolvency was not a credible threat"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-010:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The reliance on a public bail-out without the threat of any significant losses shared by management, shareholders and creditors may have created an asymmetric incentive for excessive risk taking by financial institutions.",
      "text_sha256": "715440667f9a4d96465a931394c448d06f6d2c8f43a318447d6e849a44a3d1ca",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-012",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-012",
      "text": "The German provision allowing appropriate compensation of shareholders whose rights are impaired can defeat the statute's own purpose, because time is of the essence in bank reorganization and the appointment of a court-appointed expert to value shareholder claims may significantly slow the procedure.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "systemic-risk",
        "corporate-governance",
        "economics"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "reorganization procedure under the German Financial Institution Reorganization Act",
        "where shareholders' rights are permanently impaired by the plan"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-012:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Time is of the essence in any bank reorganization and the appointment of an expert may significantly slow down the procedure, making it less effective.",
      "text_sha256": "b485b1e251b4fd6556b7abefd1413a2a274622432d10cbf1054b741f230fdb82",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-014",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-014",
      "text": "Because German law fixes no threshold conditions or determining factors for market reception or market confidence, the systemic relevance and contagion determinations that turn on those factors can never be made in a reliable and objective manner.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "systemic-risk",
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "economics",
        "law-and-legal-systems"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "determination of systemic threat under the German Banking Act"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-014:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The German law does not stipulate any threshold conditions or determining factors for market reception or confidence. As a result, it will always be difficult to determine any of these factors in a reliable and objective manner.",
      "text_sha256": "2145c8eca7a7bc901ee82aa5fabc8b62155accc69f8ee96ef6a0366e6c3b14b2",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-021",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-021",
      "text": "Contingent capital supports general risk control and reduces moral hazard by holding shareholders responsible and internalizing the costs of bank failure rather than externalizing them onto taxpayers.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "corporate-governance"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 12
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-021:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Contingent capital may also support general risk control in financial institutions454 and may contribute to minimizing moral hazard by holding shareholders responsible and internalizing bank failure costs.",
      "text_sha256": "53c33940c4803a7130cbe584f1310f49cfabdbd3fdd646e79d0bbc333180cc8d",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-022",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-022",
      "text": "Because conversion carries a threat of loss and implicit dilution of stock holdings, contingent capital reduces shareholders' incentive to push management toward higher risk in pursuit of higher returns.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "corporate-governance"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "conversion terms that dilute existing equity holders"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 12
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-022:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Moreover, given the threat of loss due to conversion and the implicit dilution of stock holdings, contingent capital has the potential to reduce incentives for shareholders to encourage management to take higher risks for higher returns.",
      "text_sha256": "72f4e46d6eaf3d09fe0c38956384752500747223accdd975a4a5a96706ce8c02",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-029",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-029",
      "text": "Building critical mass in the contingent capital securities market could require banks and other financial institutions to buy their competitors' contingent capital securities, which would raise ethical, antitrust and incentive concerns.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "reputation",
        "economics",
        "systemic-risk",
        "risk-and-incentives"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "where non-bank demand is insufficient to absorb issuance volumes"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 40
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-029:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Developing a critical mass for the market in contingent capital securities could require banks and other financial institutions to purchase their competitors' contingent capital securities.533 That could raise ethical, antitrust, and incentive concerns.",
      "text_sha256": "9d13dd182b295987b18566083b476a2ee7aac5fa6ddc3ef7a3a080d546ee7e9f",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-034",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2061166-034",
      "text": "A second, sequential trigger placed before reorganization or resolution cushions the risk that policy makers misstructure the first trigger, absorbing the negative effects of inadequate or untimely conversion at the moment the institution needs capital.",
      "claim_type": "design",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "systemic-risk",
        "governance-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "contingent capital with sequential triggers spanning going and gone concern",
        "where the first trigger proves unsuccessful"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2061166-034:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Given the risk that policy makers may not structure the trigger appropriately, the negative effects of inadequate or untimely conversion of debt into equity at a time when the company requires a capital injection could be cushioned with a second trigger",
      "text_sha256": "06bf33727d0f8b5aabad2f40f9628f92117fa19dacbde327ff1d90f6a207f017",
      "source_sha256": "43625ea260d0fc045e86d3435df50c9d0ca4abf8542e6085a6165be6653b4a7b",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2097160-001",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2097160-001",
      "text": "Early European initiatives to put contingent convertible bonds into executive pay lack governance-improving designs; contingent convertible bonds with an early conversion trigger should be used in executive compensation instead.",
      "claim_type": "design",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "corporate-governance",
        "governance-design",
        "systemic-risk"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "systemically important financial institutions",
        "executive compensation packages"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2097160-001:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Early initiatives by European SIFIs to include contingent convertible bonds in executive compensation packages lack governance-improving designs. This Article suggests the use of contingent convertible bonds with an early conversion trigger in executive compensation.",
      "text_sha256": "36a320a5ecf8dc3bcc6db8f19b8916a37e20f5145d87d97cd0a78b512ab20885",
      "source_sha256": "1e1f8aa246bce19f4658dbceb455c7c2a272aa5d63a9e9bdaa46a3a5a87680fc",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2097160-003",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2097160-003",
      "text": "Contingent convertible bonds placed in executive compensation serve a different purpose than those sold to investors: the point is not capital infusion during a crisis but governance-improving design that optimizes management incentives.",
      "claim_type": "definitional",
      "topics": [
        "contingent-capital",
        "corporate-governance",
        "governance-design",
        "risk-and-incentives"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "contingent convertible bonds held by executives rather than outside investors"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 20
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2097160-003:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "bonds issued to investors, the emphasis for contingent convertible bonds in executive compensation is not on a capital infusion when the Systematically Important Financial Institution (SIFI) is in a crisis,13 but rather on governance-improving designs to help optimize management's incentives.14",
      "text_sha256": "aaaf0e9d312599577b1d47ba9322e3920718eafcb9a75f4e973a75f99aafb446",
      "source_sha256": "1e1f8aa246bce19f4658dbceb455c7c2a272aa5d63a9e9bdaa46a3a5a87680fc",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2097160-005",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2097160-005",
      "text": "Market solutions and private ordering alone are unlikely to produce contingent capital designs that improve corporate governance in SIFIs, because privately negotiated sales so far have not produced governance-sensitive designs.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "contingent-capital",
        "governance-design",
