      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "evidenced",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2748096-035:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "They find that the outperformance previously attributed to predictability in managerial skills disappears in hedge fund portfolios when accounting for liquidity risk.",
      "text_sha256": "17013dd135c6bd332b1489f285451b41ed92fbb1fa563feb88894d01303c43c7",
      "source_sha256": "8f30260f2c1db728b45c4f3b9b7c64358cf9d3217277bc3c63a910c32f87b508",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2748096-036",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2748096-036",
      "text": "Risk measures that are not adjusted for serial correlation in hedge fund returns can considerably underestimate the true extent of both individual and systemic hedge fund risk, so empirical work in this area must account for autocorrelation.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "research-methods",
        "risk-and-incentives",
        "economics"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "applies to empirical studies of hedge fund returns"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "evidenced",
      "relevance_score": 10
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2748096-036:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "unadjusted risk measures can considerably underestimate the true extent of individual and systemic risks for hedge funds.",
      "text_sha256": "03d6ec9c9d55f9503ecb275ee87e15eed7ce6d7bf4e6f1242774dfaefa9d70e3",
      "source_sha256": "8f30260f2c1db728b45c4f3b9b7c64358cf9d3217277bc3c63a910c32f87b508",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-005",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-005",
      "text": "Dynamic regulation is defined as conceptualizing regulatory phenomena in relation to both preceding and succeeding events, using institution-specific and decentralized information to generate feedback effects that support anticipatory rulemaking.",
      "claim_type": "definitional",
      "topics": [
        "dynamic-regulation",
        "institutional-design",
        "decentralization"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "asserted",
      "relevance_score": 20
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-005:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The theory of dynamic regulation conceptualizes the study of regulatory phenomena in relation to preceding and succeeding events, using institution-specific and decentralized information to facilitate feedback effects for anticipatory rulemaking.105",
      "text_sha256": "511612218d90e5180e1ebd42d30779750910aa61f2fa5be4933700cd1e364d6f",
      "source_sha256": "3515f2317ed9c1d33ae55d70467cdf1f6c4e350065ea6a887d5d7619599ad7d2",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-006",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-006",
      "text": "Data derived from venture capital investments can function as a dynamic regulatory supplement for disruptive innovation, because venture capital's financial allocations to innovative projects supply feedback for dynamic regulation.",
      "claim_type": "design",
      "topics": [
        "innovation",
        "dynamic-regulation",
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "where venture capital investment allocation data is available to regulators",
        "United States venture capital market, 2005 to 2015"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "evidenced",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-006:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "We show that data derived from venture capital investments can function as a dynamic regulatory supplement for disruptive innovation. Venture capital's financial allocations to innovative projects can provide feedback for dynamic regulation of",
      "text_sha256": "6ef1b3c2661d71f7247c8080dd21cca5e0d50bb2557d95980be988d39694e881",
      "source_sha256": "3515f2317ed9c1d33ae55d70467cdf1f6c4e350065ea6a887d5d7619599ad7d2",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-009",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-009",
      "text": "Because artificial intelligence is not recognized as a subject of law in national or international law, it has no legal personality and therefore cannot be personally liable for damages it causes.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "ai-and-agents",
        "law-and-legal-systems"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "current national and international legal frameworks",
        "autonomous AI acting without supervision"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 35
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-009:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "provisions in the existing regulatory framework. However, national and international law do not (currently) recognize AI as a subject of law. Without legal personality, AI cannot be personally liable for damages.63 With autonomous AI playing an expanding role in",
      "text_sha256": "a3da951c937fdfd4ccc1e3f21a4e71c4cfd02133b68f1144bcfd3a3c9a1ee399",
      "source_sha256": "3515f2317ed9c1d33ae55d70467cdf1f6c4e350065ea6a887d5d7619599ad7d2",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2808132-020",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2808132-020",
      "text": "The historically evolved rulemaking infrastructure could cope with its own collective action problems, regulatory cycles, and trial-and-error rulemaking without major disruption because the scope of regulatory issues was limited and institutional designs were consensus driven.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "small, stable, less interconnected societies with limited upward mobility",
        "limited and readily identifiable sets of regulatory issues"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
