    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-032",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2831040-032",
      "text": "Anticipatory rulemaking in the dynamic framework is accomplished by combining institution specific, decentralized, and timely information with feedback effects, which can occur between public and private rulemakers, between outcomes and institutions, across jurisdictions, and between rules and rulemaking processes.",
      "claim_type": "design",
      "topics": [
        "dynamic-regulation",
        "institutional-design",
        "decentralization"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 22
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-032:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "It relies on the use of institution-specific, decentralized, and timely information5 in combination with feedback effects (Kaal 2014b). Feedback effects can occur in several settings including feedback processes between different public and private rulemakers",
      "text_sha256": "7c08ee8cc32ecb6fc364c4b86625844612e91e4164fbd9eac0a0d0690e94975f",
      "source_sha256": "de5156f14f44a5753cab55b6c4b03049eda58a143b99e08ef427aee1d261e1bf",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-034",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2831040-034",
      "text": "Dynamic regulatory tools lower unforeseen contingencies in innovation related rulemaking because their feedback effects supply relevant, timely, decentralized, and institution specific information ex ante, which also helps maintain certainty in the rulemaking process.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "law-and-legal-systems",
        "dynamic-regulation"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-034:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "By increasing the availability of information ex-ante, dynamic regulatory tools help lower unforeseen contingencies in the rulemaking process pertaining to innovation. Improved information for rulemaking also helps maintaining certainty in the rulemaking process.",
      "text_sha256": "f7329ff8c78dc5f942eaf006d3ed7d365c0324bb89da73dd92a430731cffdde8",
      "source_sha256": "de5156f14f44a5753cab55b6c4b03049eda58a143b99e08ef427aee1d261e1bf",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-036",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2831040-036",
      "text": "Rules operate as a feedback effect on the rulemaking process itself: rules with suboptimal characteristics result from institutional arrangements and then reinforce those suboptimal arrangements, and stable presumptively optimal rules reinforce an institutional structure that perpetuates stability in rules.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design",
        "dynamic-regulation"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-036:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Existing rules create a feedback effect for the rulemaking process itself. Rules with suboptimal characteristics are the results of institutional arrangements and reinforce suboptimal institutional arrangements and rulemaking processes.",
      "text_sha256": "22f9168478a9a51f146ce32af1f9d70914b0f4f2aca706718f4f6431a2a1f4e5",
      "source_sha256": "de5156f14f44a5753cab55b6c4b03049eda58a143b99e08ef427aee1d261e1bf",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:2831040-037",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/2831040-037",
      "text": "Consumer choice adds a dynamic element to rulemaking because once consumers opt out of a suboptimal regulatory regime, public rulemakers in that jurisdiction can adjust their rulemaking in response, creating a feedback effect for the public rulemaker.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "economics",
        "institutional-design",
        "law-and-legal-systems"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "jurisdictions where consumers can opt out of a regulatory regime"
