    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-001:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "The concept of dynamic regulation in this article describes a supplemental regulatory tool that increases the availability of relevant, institution-specific, and decentralized information for rulemaking through feedback effects",
      "text_sha256": "887ddab2fd45aa7ad28b23d7aa1acb3fc2f7dfb67ec988862b1e40f496695ba7",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-012",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-012",
      "text": "Interaction and exchange of emerging information between public and private rulemakers creates a feedback process that increases the availability, timeliness, and quality of information available to the public rulemaker.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design",
        "dynamic-regulation"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "public and private rulemakers actually exchange emerging information"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-012:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Interaction between public rulemakers and private rulemakers, including the exchange of emerging information, creates a feed- back process between them that increases the availability, timeliness, and quality of information available to the public rulemaker.",
      "text_sha256": "b3c98ae741c52cea0259055d5ea7466def7a60fc0812454ff4401c65e30e2b2f",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-014",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-014",
      "text": "Feedback effects allow the necessary information to be acquired ex ante and necessary revisions to be anticipated before rules emerge as suboptimal, rather than ex post after failure has become apparent.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "dynamic-regulation",
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "feedback processes are operating before enactment"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-014:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Rather than acquiring the necessary information ex-post after rules have emerged as suboptimal, feedback effects help increase the availability of rele- vant information for rulemaking ex-ante and anticipate necessary revisions before rules emerge as suboptimal",
      "text_sha256": "30f951b082cc6f479b70f9ee8fb398cda415a16c0a4c7cb9276a0236d675f7e8",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-020",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-020",
      "text": "Contrary to the dominant view of corporate governance as a forward looking endeavor, dynamic governance structures are properly categorized as backward looking ex ante forms of corporate governance.",
      "claim_type": "definitional",
      "topics": [
        "governance-design",
        "corporate-governance",
        "dynamic-regulation"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "within the theoretical framework of dynamic regulation"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-020:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "By contrast, in the theoretical framework of dynamic regulation, dynamic gov- ernance structures can be categorized as backward-looking ex-ante forms of corporate governance.",
      "text_sha256": "0473bd8b6c1017af7a02347dcd1e73c100dcee6ba3278aa2b606beb79d59337e",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-021",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-021",
      "text": "Traditional forward looking corporate governance systems adopt backward looking perspectives only after stable and optimal rules have emerged as suboptimal and require replacement or amendment.",
      "claim_type": "failure",
      "topics": [
        "governance-design",
        "corporate-governance"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "traditional stable and presumptively optimal rulemaking infrastructure"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-021:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Traditional forward-looking corporate governance systems may take backward-looking perspectives if and when stable and optimal rules emerge as suboptimal, requiring replacement and/or amendment of existing regulatory solutions.",
      "text_sha256": "587ace8b800e4b2f3639aa13bfe6749afebe3a4e1af161b3bde597812499700c",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-023",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-023",
      "text": "Targeted use of governmental contracts allows the government to successfully reform corporate governance not only in individual public corporations but across entire industries.",
      "claim_type": "empirical",
      "topics": [
        "governance-design",
        "corporate-governance",
        "compliance"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "evidenced",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-023:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Empirical evidence suggests that the targeted use of governmental con- tracts allows the government to successfully reform corporate governance in public corporations and entire industries70.",
      "text_sha256": "d5ac6db0ba8d7ccd05892e0411ee6c30da03e6fc27667c52e690469578f97589",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-024",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-024",
      "text": "Over 97 percent of the non and deferred prosecution agreements executed in the United States between 1993 and 2013 contained governance changes, including required business changes in 30 percent and board and senior management changes in 38 percent.",
      "claim_type": "empirical",
      "topics": [
        "compliance",
        "governance-design",
        "corporate-governance",
        "empirical-evidence"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "United States N/DPAs executed between 1993 and 2013"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "evidenced",
      "relevance_score": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-024:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Over 97% of the N/DPAs executed in the United States between 1993 and 2013 contained governance changes, including required business changes (30%), board and senior management changes (38%",
      "text_sha256": "738e83f79c1185384bd5a447924d73106a60d40f3d16ddc9ab1601d5243d696b",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-025",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-025",
      "text": "The governance improvements documented in governmental contracts counteract or at least discount the standard criticisms of them, including unequal bargaining power, the government's lack of governance expertise, and the potential for prosecutorial abuse.",
      "claim_type": "empirical",
      "topics": [
        "governance-design",
        "corporate-governance"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-025:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Concerns that may be dis- counted include the unequal bargaining power between the contracting par- ties78, the government's lack of governance expertise and training79, the po- tential for prosecutorial abuse80",
      "text_sha256": "2679ce8e4dc9bf079f671d1b1a319e918acdeecb8e88da125b337a94f7756d6e",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-035",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-035",
      "text": "Negotiation of a governmental contract produces indirect information transfers, because the government can deduce from where the corporate wrongdoer places negotiating emphasis which governance shortcomings most need remedying.",
      "claim_type": "mechanism",
      "topics": [
        "institutional-design"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [
        "the parties negotiate contract terms rather than accept imposed terms"
      ],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 22
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-035:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Indirect information transfers and feedback effects may exist because the government may be able to deduce from the emphasis of the corporate wrongdoer's negotiations what particular governance shortcomings need par- ticular remedying.",
      "text_sha256": "e16823d771bddc19318951f24773eab2d5a830f86eecba476138f6574448cf29",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-040",
      "type": "Claim",
      "canonical_url": "https://wulfkaal.github.io/claims/kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-040",
      "text": "Dynamic governance structures involving governmental contracts are backward looking because the multilevel feedback effects they generate require an exchange of information about past events in order to anticipate future regulatory contingencies.",
      "claim_type": "definitional",
      "topics": [
        "governance-design",
        "dynamic-regulation"
      ],
      "scope_conditions": [],
      "attribution_class": "source_derived",
      "attribution_status": "directly_attributable_to_wulf_a_kaal",
      "verification_status": "current",
      "source_quote_status": "present_in_canonical_claim_record",
      "source_quote_individual_match_status": "not_recomputed_by_pilot",
      "confidence": "argued",
      "relevance_score": 24
    },
    {
      "id": "kaal:claim:kaal-2014-dynamicregulationviagove-040:source-passage",
      "type": "SourcePassage",
      "text": "Dynamic governance structures involving governmental contracts are back- ward-looking because multilevel feedback effects associated with govern- mental contracts necessitate an exchange of information pertaining to past events to anticipate future regulatory contingencies.",
      "text_sha256": "970ab72d7bceb5b45ef09d782874aa4a7a479f65de786e6065963b196d2b638e",
      "source_sha256": "7320aec036ccf2f6739f0854ea8749903e8902e77c2ec39543b64b358459c408",
      "locator_status": "exact_quote_without_page_locator"
    },
